In this lesson we'll look at what stays the same and what changes when you enlarge a shape. First, the stuff that stays the same. An enlargement keeps the shape exactly the same, just bigger or smaller. So all the angles are unchanged, parallel lines stay parallel, and a circle stays a circle. That's why we call the new shape similar to the original.

Now, the stuff that changes. Every length gets multiplied by the size of the scale factor, which we call k. So if k is three, every side becomes three times as long. Areas scale up much faster, because area is length times length, so each area gets multiplied by k squared. Imagine a little square one centimetre by one centimetre. When you enlarge it by k, you can fit k by k copies of that little square inside, giving an area of k squared. Volumes scale even faster, by k cubed, because you're filling a cube with k by k by k tiny cubes. So lengths grow by k, areas by k squared, and volumes by k cubed.

Now let's see it in action.